# Physics 9 - written explanations (round 3)
@phys9-u1-q01
Q: What is the SI unit of mass?
A: The kilogram (kg) – not the gram.
W: The seven SI base units include the metre (length), kilogram (mass) and second (time). | The kilogram is the only base unit that already carries a prefix (kilo = 1000), so the base unit is kg, not g. | Answer: kilogram, symbol kg (small k, no plural s).
T: Write kg, never Kg or kgs – capital K is the kelvin.
@phys9-u1-q02
Q: Is speed a fundamental (base) quantity? Explain.
A: No – speed is a derived quantity: distance (length) ÷ time.
W: Fundamental quantities cannot be defined from other quantities (length, mass, time, current, temperature, amount, luminous intensity). | Speed = distance ÷ time, so it is built from length and time. | Its unit m/s is made from m and s, so speed is derived, not fundamental.
T: If a quantity's unit is a combination of base units (m/s, kg/m³, N), it is derived.
@phys9-u1-q05
Q: Write 0.0045 in scientific notation.
A: 4.5 × 10⁻³
W: Scientific notation is a × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ a < 10. | Move the decimal point right until one non-zero digit is in front: 0.0045 → 4.5 (3 places). | Moving right 3 places means n = −3, so 0.0045 = 4.5 × 10⁻³.
T: Small numbers (less than 1) always have a negative power of ten.
@phys9-u1-q06
Q: How many significant figures are in 0.00450?
A: 3 (the 4, the 5 and the final 0)
W: Leading zeros (0.00…) only place the decimal point – they are not significant. | Non-zero digits 4 and 5 are significant. | A trailing zero after the decimal point is significant, so 0.00450 has 3 significant figures.
T: Count from the first non-zero digit; zeros at the end of a decimal count.
@phys9-u1-q07
Q: Add 12.3 + 0.45 and give the answer to the correct precision.
A: 12.8
W: In addition, the answer keeps as many decimal places as the least precise number. | 12.3 has 1 decimal place, 0.45 has 2, so the answer gets 1 decimal place. | 12.3 + 0.45 = 12.75 → rounded to one decimal place = 12.8.
T: Adding/subtracting: count decimal places. Multiplying/dividing: count significant figures.
@phys9-u1-q08
Q: What is the difference between accuracy and precision?
A: Accuracy = how close a measurement is to the true value; precision = how close repeated measurements are to each other.
W: Accuracy compares the measurement with the true (accepted) value. | Precision compares repeated readings with one another – small spread means high precision. | Readings can be precise but not accurate (all close together but far from the true value, e.g. a zero error).
T: Think of darts: accurate = near the bull's-eye; precise = darts grouped tightly.
@phys9-u1-q09
Q: Give an example of a scalar quantity.
A: Temperature, mass, time, speed or energy (any one).
W: A scalar has magnitude (size) only, no direction. | Mass (5 kg), time (10 s), temperature (25 °C) and energy (100 J) are complete without a direction. | So any of these is a scalar.
T: Ask: does "which way?" make sense? If not, it is a scalar.
@phys9-u1-q10
Q: Give an example of a vector quantity.
A: Displacement, velocity, acceleration or force (any one).
W: A vector has both magnitude and direction. | Displacement (5 m north), velocity (20 m/s east) and force (10 N down) all need a direction. | So any of these is a vector.
T: Vectors are drawn as arrows: length = size, arrowhead = direction.
@phys9-u1-q11
Q: A person walks 10 m east and then 10 m west. What are the distance travelled and the displacement?
A: Distance = 20 m; displacement = 0 m.
W: Distance is the total path length: 10 m + 10 m = 20 m. | Displacement is the change in position from start to finish (a vector). | The person ends where they started, so displacement = 0 m.
T: Displacement can never be larger than the distance travelled.
@phys9-u1-q12
Q: A 5 N force acts along 0° (east) and another 5 N force acts along 90° (north). Find the resultant.
A: 5√2 N ≈ 7.1 N at 45° (north-east)
W: The forces are perpendicular, so use Pythagoras: R = √(F₁² + F₂²). | R = √(5² + 5²) = √50 = 5√2 ≈ 7.1 N. | Direction: tan θ = 5/5 = 1, so θ = 45° from east.
T: Perpendicular vectors: Pythagoras for size, tan θ = opposite/adjacent for direction.
@phys9-u1-q13
Q: A 10 N force acts at 30° to the x-axis. Find its x-component.
A: Fₓ = 10 cos 30° = 5√3 ≈ 8.7 N
W: Component along the axis the angle is measured from: Fₓ = F cos θ. | Substitute: Fₓ = 10 N × cos 30° = 10 × 0.866. | Fₓ ≈ 8.7 N (= 5√3 N).
T: cos goes with the angle's own axis; sin with the other axis (F_y = F sin θ = 5 N here).
@phys9-u1-q15
Q: What is the dimension of speed?
A: [L][T]⁻¹
W: Speed = distance ÷ time. | Distance has dimension [L], time has dimension [T]. | So speed = [L]/[T] = [L][T]⁻¹.
T: Dimensions ignore numbers and units – only M, L, T (and others) remain.
@phys9-u1-q16
Q: What is the dimension of force?
A: [M][L][T]⁻²
W: F = ma. | Mass = [M]; acceleration = m/s² = [L][T]⁻². | F = [M] × [L][T]⁻² = [M][L][T]⁻².
T: Build dimensions from a formula you know (F = ma, W = Fs, P = W/t).
@phys9-u1-q17
Q: Convert 2.5 h to seconds.
A: 9000 s
W: 1 h = 60 min and 1 min = 60 s, so 1 h = 3600 s. | 2.5 h × 3600 s/h. | = 9000 s.
T: Write the conversion factor as a fraction so the unwanted unit cancels.
@phys9-u1-q18
Q: What power of ten does the prefix micro (µ) stand for?
A: 10⁻⁶
W: Small prefixes: milli (m) = 10⁻³, micro (µ) = 10⁻⁶, nano (n) = 10⁻⁹. | So 1 µm = 10⁻⁶ m. | Answer: micro = 10⁻⁶.
T: Each step milli → micro → nano is a further factor of 1000.
@phys9-u1-q19
Q: Why do scientists use the SI system of units?
A: It is agreed worldwide, so results from different countries can be compared.
W: A system of units must be universally agreed and based on reproducible standards. | SI gives one base unit for each fundamental quantity. | Everyone uses the same units, so measurements can be shared and compared without conversion mistakes.
T: SI = Système International – "international" is the key word.
@phys9-u1-q20
Q: What is parallax error when reading a scale?
A: An error caused by reading the scale from an angle instead of straight on.
W: The pointer or liquid surface is a small distance in front of the scale. | Looking from above or below makes it line up with the wrong mark. | Avoid it by keeping your eye perpendicular to the scale at the reading point.
T: Eye level, straight on – every time.
@phys9-u1-q21
Q: What is the SI unit of density?
A: kg/m³ (kg m⁻³)
W: Density ρ = mass ÷ volume. | SI units: mass in kg, volume in m³. | So ρ is measured in kg/m³ (1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³).
T: Get the unit from the formula: divide the units just as you divide the quantities.
@phys9-u1-q23
Q: How is the vector −A related to vector A?
A: Same magnitude, opposite direction.
W: Multiplying a vector by −1 keeps its length. | It reverses the arrow's direction. | So A + (−A) = 0.
T: Subtracting B means adding −B: A − B = A + (−B).
@phys9-u1-q24
Q: Can three forces of 3 N each add up to zero? How?
A: Yes – if they are 120° apart.
W: Forces add to zero when their arrows, joined head to tail, form a closed shape. | Three equal 3 N arrows form a closed equilateral triangle. | Each interior angle is 60°, so the forces point 120° apart and the resultant is 0.
T: Zero resultant = closed vector polygon.
@phys9-u1-q25
Q: What is the difference between position and displacement?
A: Position is where an object is measured from an origin; displacement is the change in position.
W: The position vector points from the chosen origin to the object. | Displacement = final position − initial position. | Displacement does not depend on where the origin is; position does.
T: Displacement Δx = x_final − x_initial.
@phys9-u1-q27
Q: Round 2.45 to 2 significant figures (using the usual "5 rounds up" rule).
A: 2.5
W: Keep the first two significant digits: 2.4|5. | The next digit is 5, so round up. | 2.45 → 2.5.
T: Some books use "round to even" (giving 2.4) – follow your teacher's rule in exams.
@phys9-u1-q28
Q: How do you add two vectors graphically using the parallelogram method?
A: Draw both from the same point, complete the parallelogram; the diagonal from that point is the resultant.
W: Draw A and B to scale with their tails at the same point O. | Draw lines parallel to each vector to complete the parallelogram. | The diagonal from O to the opposite corner is the resultant R; measure its length and angle.
T: Parallelogram = tails together; triangle method = head to tail. Both give the same R.
@phys9-u1-q29
Q: What is a quantity with no direction called?
A: A scalar.
W: Physical quantities are either scalars or vectors. | A scalar has size only (e.g. mass, time, energy). | So a quantity with no direction is a scalar.
T: Do not draw an arrow on a scalar.
@phys9-u1-q30
Q: Trap: a student says "5 m/s north + 5 m/s south = 10 m/s". What is wrong?
A: Velocities are vectors in opposite directions, so they cancel: resultant = 0 m/s.
W: Take north as +: +5 m/s and −5 m/s. | Vector sum = 5 + (−5) = 0 m/s. | 10 m/s is the sum of speeds (magnitudes), not the vector sum.
T: Before adding vectors, choose a + direction and give each one its sign.
@phys9-u2-q01
Q: What is the difference between speed and velocity?
A: Speed is how fast (scalar); velocity is speed in a given direction (vector).
W: Speed = distance ÷ time – size only. | Velocity = displacement ÷ time – size and direction. | A car going round a bend at a steady 20 m/s has constant speed but changing velocity.
T: Velocity changes if either the speed or the direction changes.
@phys9-u2-q02
Q: A runner covers 100 m in 20 s. What is the average speed?
A: 5 m/s
W: Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. | = 100 m ÷ 20 s. | = 5 m/s.
T: Always divide the TOTAL distance by the TOTAL time.
@phys9-u2-q06
Q: An object is dropped from rest from a height of 20 m (g = 10 m/s²). How long does it take to reach the ground?
A: t = 2 s
W: Use s = ut + ½gt² with u = 0. | 20 m = 0 + ½ × 10 m/s² × t² = 5t². | t² = 4 s², so t = 2 s.
T: "Dropped" means u = 0, which removes the ut term.
@phys9-u2-q07
Q: The object dropped from 20 m falls for 2 s (g = 10 m/s²). What is its speed just before it hits the ground?
A: v = 20 m/s
W: Use v = u + gt. | u = 0, g = 10 m/s², t = 2 s. | v = 0 + 10 × 2 = 20 m/s. Check: v² = 2gs = 2 × 10 × 20 = 400, v = 20 m/s ✓.
T: Check a free-fall answer with a second suvat equation.
@phys9-u2-q09
Q: A ball is thrown straight up at 20 m/s (g = 10 m/s²). What maximum height does it reach?
A: 20 m
W: At the top, v = 0. Use v² = u² − 2gh. | 0 = (20 m/s)² − 2 × 10 m/s² × h. | 20h = 400, so h = 20 m.
T: Maximum height always means v = 0 at the top.
@phys9-u2-q10
Q: What does the distance–time (s–t) graph look like for uniform velocity?
A: A straight sloping line; its slope (gradient) is the velocity.
W: Uniform velocity means equal distances in equal times. | So s increases by the same amount each second – a straight line. | Gradient = Δs/Δt = velocity; a steeper line means a larger velocity.
T: s–t graph: slope = velocity. Horizontal line = at rest.
@phys9-u2-q11
Q: What does the velocity–time (v–t) graph look like for uniform acceleration?
A: A straight sloping line; slope = acceleration and area under it = displacement.
W: Uniform acceleration means v changes by the same amount each second. | So the v–t graph is a straight line with gradient Δv/Δt = a. | The area between the line and the time axis gives the displacement.
T: v–t graph: slope = a, area = s.
@phys9-u2-q12
Q: An object starts from rest and then has constant acceleration. What shape is its s–t graph?
A: A curve (parabola) that starts flat and gets steeper.
W: s = ½at² – s depends on t², not t. | At t = 0 the velocity is 0, so the slope starts at zero (flat). | As v increases, the slope increases, giving an upward-curving parabola.
T: Curving up on an s–t graph = speeding up.
@phys9-u2-q13
Q: How do you find instantaneous speed from an s–t graph?
A: Find the slope of the tangent at that instant.
W: Instantaneous speed is the speed at one moment. | Draw a tangent touching the curve at that time. | Slope of the tangent = Δs/Δt = instantaneous speed.
T: Average speed uses a chord between two points; instantaneous uses a tangent.
@phys9-u2-q14
Q: What does a negative velocity mean?
A: The object moves in the direction opposite to the one chosen as positive.
W: Velocity is a vector; its sign shows direction along a line. | If right/up is chosen as +, a negative velocity means moving left/down. | The sign says nothing about speeding up or slowing down by itself.
T: State your + direction before solving.
@phys9-u2-q15
Q: What does an acceleration of zero mean?
A: The velocity is constant (which could be zero – at rest).
W: a = Δv/Δt. | If a = 0, then Δv = 0: the velocity does not change. | The object moves at constant velocity or stays at rest.
T: a = 0 does not mean v = 0.
@phys9-u2-q16
Q: What is a frame of reference?
A: The point of view (origin and axes) from which position and motion are measured.
W: Motion is always measured relative to something. | A frame of reference fixes an origin, directions and a clock. | A passenger is at rest relative to the bus but moving relative to the road.
T: Always ask: "moving relative to what?"
@phys9-u2-q17
Q: A car moving at 20 m/s brakes with acceleration −4 m/s². What is its stopping distance?
A: 50 m
W: Use v² = u² + 2as with v = 0. | 0 = (20 m/s)² + 2 × (−4 m/s²) × s. | 8s = 400, so s = 50 m.
T: When time is not given or asked, use v² = u² + 2as.
@phys9-u2-q18
Q: For the same car (20 m/s, a = −4 m/s²), how long does it take to stop?
A: 5 s
W: Use v = u + at. | 0 = 20 m/s + (−4 m/s²) × t. | 4t = 20, so t = 5 s.
T: Check: average speed 10 m/s × 5 s = 50 m – same as the stopping distance.
@phys9-u2-q19
Q: A v–t graph shows a constant velocity of 10 m/s from t = 0 to t = 5 s. What is the displacement?
A: 50 m
W: Displacement = area under the v–t graph. | The area is a rectangle: height 10 m/s × width 5 s. | = 50 m.
T: Area of a v–t graph = displacement (rectangles, triangles, trapezia).
@phys9-u2-q20
Q: In up-and-down (vertical) motion problems, what must you choose first?
A: A positive direction – e.g. up = +, so g = −10 m/s².
W: Velocity, displacement and acceleration are vectors with signs. | If up is +, then g acts down, so a = −10 m/s². | Keep the same sign rule for every quantity in the problem.
T: One sign convention per problem – write it at the top.
@phys9-u2-q21
Q: Why is average velocity Δs/Δt, and not the average of the speeds, when an object turns back?
A: Because velocity uses displacement, which can be small (even zero) when the object returns.
W: Average velocity = total displacement ÷ total time. | When the object turns back, part of the motion cancels, so displacement < distance. | Averaging speeds ignores direction and gives a wrong (too big) value.
T: Round trip back to start: average velocity = 0, average speed > 0.
@phys9-u2-q22
Q: What does "free fall" ignore in this chapter?
A: Air resistance – only gravity acts.
W: Free fall means the only force is the object's weight. | Then every object accelerates at g ≈ 9.8 m/s² (≈ 10 m/s²), whatever its mass. | Air resistance is ignored in these problems.
T: In free fall, a feather and a hammer land together (as on the Moon).
@phys9-u2-q23
Q: An object is thrown up. Compare its velocity when it passes the launch height on the way down with its launch velocity.
A: Same size, opposite direction (sign).
W: Without air resistance the motion is symmetric. | Using v² = u² − 2gs with s = 0 (back at launch height) gives v² = u². | So |v| = u, but now it points down: v = −u.
T: Time up = time down, speed up = speed down (no air resistance).
@phys9-u2-q24
Q: What are the SI units of acceleration?
A: m/s² (m s⁻²)
W: a = Δv ÷ Δt. | Units: (m/s) ÷ s. | = m/s².
T: "Metres per second, per second" – velocity changes by so many m/s each second.
@phys9-u2-q26
Q: Convert 72 km/h to m/s.
A: 20 m/s
W: 1 km/h = 1000 m ÷ 3600 s = 1/3.6 m/s. | 72 km/h ÷ 3.6. | = 20 m/s.
T: km/h → m/s: divide by 3.6; m/s → km/h: multiply by 3.6.
@phys9-u2-q28
Q: Trap: why is it wrong to use s = vt when the acceleration is not zero?
A: s = vt only works with constant velocity; with acceleration you need the average velocity or a suvat equation.
W: When a ≠ 0, v keeps changing, so no single v fits s = vt. | Use s = ((u + v)/2) × t or s = ut + ½at². | Using the final v gives a distance that is too big.
T: Accelerating? Reach for a suvat equation, not s = vt.
@phys9-u2-q29
Q: Trap: an object is dropped, but a student uses u = 10 m/s. What is wrong?
A: A dropped object starts from rest, so u = 0 (10 m/s² is g, not u).
W: "Dropped" or "released" means initial velocity u = 0. | 10 is the value of g (an acceleration, m/s²), not a velocity. | Put u = 0 and a = g = 10 m/s².
T: Check units: u is in m/s, g in m/s² – do not mix them up.
@phys9-u2-q30
Q: You need three suvat quantities to find a fourth. What must you never do if one is missing?
A: Never invent (guess) a value – find another equation or use the given information (e.g. "from rest" means u = 0).
W: Each suvat equation links four of s, u, v, a, t. | List the known quantities and the unknown one, then choose the equation without the quantity you do not need. | Hidden data: "from rest" u = 0; "stops" v = 0; "free fall" a = g.
T: Write s, u, v, a, t in a column and fill in what you know first.
@phys9-u3-q01
Q: State Newton's first law in one line.
A: An object stays at rest or moves at constant velocity unless a net (resultant) force acts on it.
W: Zero net force means zero acceleration. | So the velocity (speed and direction) stays constant. | A change of velocity needs a net force.
T: N1: no net force ↔ no acceleration.
@phys9-u3-q02
Q: What is inertia?
A: The tendency of an object to resist a change in its velocity; mass measures it.
W: Objects keep doing what they are doing unless a force acts (Newton's first law). | The bigger the mass, the harder it is to start, stop or turn the object. | So mass is the measure of inertia.
T: Passengers jerk forward when a bus brakes – inertia.
@phys9-u3-q04
Q: What is the weight of a 4 kg mass (g = 10 N/kg)?
A: 40 N
W: Weight W = mg. | W = 4 kg × 10 N/kg. | = 40 N.
T: Weight is a force – answer in newtons, not kg.
@phys9-u3-q05
Q: What is the difference between mass and weight?
A: Mass is the amount of matter (kg) and does not change; weight is the gravitational force on it (N) and depends on g.
W: Mass is a scalar measured in kilograms. | Weight W = mg is a force (vector, downward) measured in newtons. | Mass is the same everywhere; weight changes with g (e.g. on the Moon).
T: Balance measures mass; spring balance measures weight.
@phys9-u3-q06
Q: An astronaut's weight is smaller on the Moon. What happens to the mass?
A: The mass stays the same.
W: Mass is the amount of matter – it does not depend on place. | Weight W = mg; g on the Moon ≈ 1.6 N/kg, about 1/6 of Earth's. | So weight falls to about 1/6, mass is unchanged.
T: Only the g in W = mg changes from place to place.
@phys9-u3-q07
Q: A book rests on a table. Name the Newton's-third-law pair for the force the book exerts on the table.
A: The table pushes up on the book (normal force). The book's weight pairs with the book pulling the Earth up.
W: Third-law pairs are equal, opposite, the same type of force, and act on two different objects. | Book pushes down on table ↔ table pushes up on book (both contact forces). | Earth pulls book down (weight) ↔ book pulls Earth up (gravity).
T: Check a third-law pair: swap the two object names – "A on B" ↔ "B on A".
@phys9-u3-q08
Q: Why do Newton's-third-law forces not cancel each other?
A: Because they act on two different objects.
W: Forces cancel only when they act on the same object. | An action–reaction pair always acts on two different bodies. | Each force affects the motion of its own object.
T: To see if forces cancel, look at one object only (free-body diagram).
@phys9-u3-q09
Q: An object rests on level ground. How big is the normal force?
A: Equal to its weight (if no other vertical forces act).
W: At rest, acceleration = 0, so the net vertical force = 0. | Forces: weight mg down, normal force N up. | N − mg = 0, so N = mg.
T: Pushing down on the object or pulling it up at an angle changes N.
@phys9-u3-q10
Q: Using f_max = μN, find the maximum friction on a 5 kg block on level ground if μ = 0.4 (g = 10 m/s²).
A: N = 50 N, f_max = 20 N
W: On level ground N = mg = 5 kg × 10 m/s² = 50 N. | f_max = μN = 0.4 × 50 N. | = 20 N.
T: Find N first; friction always depends on N, not directly on mass.
@phys9-u3-q11
Q: A horizontal force of 10 N pushes the 5 kg block at rest (f_max = 20 N). Does it slide?
A: No – 10 N is less than the 20 N maximum static friction, so it stays at rest.
W: Static friction adjusts to match the push, up to f_max = 20 N. | The push is 10 N < 20 N. | So friction = 10 N, the net force is 0 and the block stays at rest.
T: The block moves only when the push exceeds f_max.
@phys9-u3-q12
Q: How does kinetic friction compare with static friction?
A: Kinetic friction acts while sliding and is usually smaller than the maximum static friction.
W: Static friction acts when surfaces do not slide and can grow up to μₛN. | Once sliding starts, kinetic friction μₖN acts. | Usually μₖ < μₛ – it is easier to keep a box moving than to start it.
T: Hard to start, easier to keep going.
@phys9-u3-q13
Q: Give ways to reduce friction.
A: Smooth/polish the surfaces, lubricate (oil, grease), use wheels or ball bearings, use an air cushion.
W: Friction comes from surfaces rubbing and gripping. | Lubricants separate the surfaces; rolling replaces sliding. | Smoother surfaces and air cushions (hovercraft) reduce contact.
T: Remember "SLOW": Smooth, Lubricate, Oil/rollers, Wheels.
@phys9-u3-q14
Q: Give examples where friction is useful.
A: Walking, brakes, gripping a pen, nails and screws holding, tyres on roads.
W: Friction opposes sliding between surfaces. | Without it, feet and tyres would slip and objects could not be held. | So friction is needed for walking, braking and holding things together.
T: Think of ice – everything that becomes hard on ice needs friction.
@phys9-u3-q16
Q: A force of 10 N acts for 0.5 s. What impulse does it give?
A: 5 N·s (equal to the change in momentum)
W: Impulse = F × Δt. | = 10 N × 0.5 s. | = 5 N·s = Δp (5 kg·m/s).
T: N·s and kg·m/s are the same unit.
@phys9-u3-q18
Q: Soft landings (bending knees, mats, airbags) increase the stopping time Δt. What happens to the average force?
A: The average force becomes smaller for the same change in momentum.
W: F = Δp ÷ Δt. | The change in momentum (stopping) is fixed. | A larger Δt gives a smaller F, so less injury.
T: Longer stopping time = gentler force.
@phys9-u3-q19
Q: Two skaters at rest push each other apart. Compare their momenta afterwards.
A: Equal in size and opposite in direction (total still zero).
W: The total momentum before is 0 (both at rest). | External forces are negligible, so momentum is conserved. | After: m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ = 0, so m₁v₁ = −m₂v₂; the lighter skater moves faster.
T: Explosions/push-apart from rest: momenta equal and opposite.
@phys9-u3-q20
Q: A 4 kg trolley at 3 m/s hits and sticks to a 2 kg trolley at rest. What is their common velocity?
A: 2 m/s
W: Momentum is conserved: m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = (m₁ + m₂)v. | 4 × 3 + 2 × 0 = 6v, so 12 = 6v. | v = 2 m/s (a perfectly inelastic collision).
T: "Stick together" = add the masses after the collision.
@phys9-u3-q21
Q: Using F = GMm/r², what is g at the Earth's surface equal to?
A: g = GM/R² ≈ 9.8 m/s² (M = Earth's mass, R = Earth's radius)
W: The weight of a mass m is mg, and it equals the gravitational force GMm/R². | mg = GMm/R², so m cancels. | g = GM/R² ≈ (6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 6.0×10²⁴)/(6.4×10⁶)² ≈ 9.8 m/s².
T: g does not depend on the falling object's mass.
@phys9-u3-q22
Q: What is the first rule when drawing a free-body diagram?
A: Draw only the forces acting ON one chosen object.
W: Isolate one object. | Draw every force on it (weight, normal, friction, tension, applied) as arrows from the object. | Leave out forces that the object exerts on other things.
T: Label each arrow with "by what on what".
@phys9-u3-q23
Q: A lift accelerates upward at 2 m/s². What is the apparent weight (normal force) of a 50 kg person in it? (g = 10 m/s²)
A: 600 N
W: Newton's second law, up = +: N − mg = ma. | N − 50 × 10 = 50 × 2, so N − 500 = 100. | N = 600 N – the person feels heavier.
T: Accelerating up: N = m(g + a). Down: N = m(g − a).
@phys9-u3-q24
Q: The same 50 kg person is in a lift accelerating downward at 2 m/s². What is the apparent weight?
A: 400 N
W: Take down = +: mg − N = ma. | 500 − N = 50 × 2 = 100. | N = 400 N – the person feels lighter.
T: In free fall (a = g) the apparent weight is zero.
@phys9-u3-q25
Q: Can an object be moving when the resultant force on it is zero?
A: Yes – it moves at constant velocity (Newton's first law).
W: Zero resultant force means zero acceleration. | Zero acceleration means the velocity does not change. | The velocity can be any constant value, not only zero.
T: Force changes motion; it is not needed to keep motion going.
@phys9-u3-q26
Q: What are the units of the coefficient of friction μ?
A: None – μ is a ratio of two forces.
W: μ = f/N. | Both f and N are in newtons. | N ÷ N cancels, so μ has no unit.
T: Ratios of the same quantity have no units.
@phys9-u3-q27
Q: The Earth pulls you down with your weight. What is the third-law pair, and why doesn't the Earth move noticeably?
A: You pull the Earth up with an equal force; the Earth's huge mass makes its acceleration tiny.
W: Third law: Earth on you ↔ you on Earth, equal and opposite. | a = F/m: the force is the same but the Earth's mass is about 6×10²⁴ kg. | So the Earth's acceleration is far too small to notice.
T: Equal forces do not mean equal accelerations.
@phys9-u3-q28
Q: Trap: "Action cancels reaction, so nothing can move." Why is this wrong?
A: Action and reaction act on different bodies, so they cannot cancel.
W: Forces cancel only on the same object. | A horse pulls the cart; the cart pulls the horse – two different objects. | Whether the cart moves depends only on the forces on the cart.
T: Draw a separate free-body diagram for each object.
@phys9-u3-q29
Q: Trap: why is it wrong to use f = μN when the block is not at the limit and not sliding?
A: Static friction is only as large as needed (f ≤ μN); μN is just the maximum.
W: Static friction balances the applied force: f = applied push while the block stays still. | It can rise only up to f_max = μN. | So f = μN only at the point of slipping (or with μₖ while sliding).
T: Not sliding? Find friction from the force balance, not from μN.
@phys9-u3-q30
Q: When can you say that the momentum p of a system is conserved?
A: When the net external force on the system is zero.
W: Δp = F_ext × Δt. | If F_ext = 0, then Δp = 0. | Check external forces such as friction or a push from the floor before using conservation.
T: Internal forces (between the objects) never change total momentum.
@phys9-u4-q01
Q: A force of 20 N moves an object 5 m in its own direction. How much work is done?
A: 100 J
W: W = Fs cos θ, with θ = 0° (cos 0° = 1). | W = 20 N × 5 m × 1. | = 100 J.
T: 1 J = 1 N·m.
@phys9-u4-q02
Q: The same 20 N force acts perpendicular to a 5 m displacement. How much work does it do?
A: 0 J
W: W = Fs cos θ with θ = 90°. | cos 90° = 0. | W = 20 × 5 × 0 = 0 J.
T: A force at right angles to the motion does no work.
@phys9-u4-q03
Q: A 20 N force acts at 60° to a 5 m displacement. How much work is done?
A: 50 J
W: W = Fs cos θ. | W = 20 N × 5 m × cos 60° = 20 × 5 × 0.5. | = 50 J.
T: Only the force component along the motion (F cos θ) does work.
@phys9-u4-q07
Q: A 2 kg mass with 100 J of potential energy at a height of 5 m is dropped. Ignoring air resistance, what is its speed just before it hits the ground? (g = 10 m/s²)
A: 10 m/s
W: Energy is conserved: PE lost = KE gained, so ½mv² = 100 J. | ½ × 2 kg × v² = 100 J, so v² = 100. | v = 10 m/s (check: v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 5) = 10 m/s).
T: The mass cancels: v = √(2gh) for any dropped object.
@phys9-u4-q08
Q: A net force of 10 N acts on a 2 kg body at rest over 4 m. Find its kinetic energy and speed.
A: KE = 40 J; v = √40 ≈ 6.3 m/s
W: Work–energy theorem: W_net = ΔKE. | W = Fs = 10 N × 4 m = 40 J, so KE = 40 J (it started at rest). | ½ × 2 × v² = 40 → v² = 40 → v ≈ 6.3 m/s.
T: Net work = change in kinetic energy.
@phys9-u4-q09
Q: What power is needed to do 100 J of work in 5 s?
A: 20 W
W: P = W ÷ t. | = 100 J ÷ 5 s. | = 20 W.
T: 1 W = 1 J/s.
@phys9-u4-q11
Q: The kWh is an energy unit. How many joules is 1 kWh?
A: 3.6 × 10⁶ J
W: E = Pt. | 1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s. | = 3 600 000 J = 3.6 × 10⁶ J.
T: Electricity bills count kWh ("units"), not joules.
@phys9-u4-q12
Q: What does negative work mean?
A: The force (or its component) acts against the motion and takes energy away, e.g. friction.
W: W = Fs cos θ. | If θ > 90°, cos θ < 0, so W < 0. | The force removes kinetic energy – friction and braking do negative work.
T: Negative work slows things down.
@phys9-u4-q13
Q: You carry a bag across a room at constant height. How much work do you do lifting it (vertically)?
A: Zero – the height does not change, so no work against gravity.
W: Your force on the bag is upward (supporting its weight). | The displacement is horizontal, at 90° to that force. | W = Fs cos 90° = 0.
T: Work needs movement along the force.
@phys9-u4-q14
Q: What does the spring PE formula ½kx² describe?
A: The elastic energy stored in a spring stretched or compressed by x.
W: Hooke's law: F = kx, so the force grows from 0 to kx. | Work done = area under the F–x graph = ½ × x × kx. | This work is stored as elastic PE = ½kx².
T: Doubling x gives four times the stored energy.
@phys9-u4-q15
Q: What does friction turn mechanical energy into?
A: Heat (internal/thermal energy) and some sound.
W: Friction opposes the sliding and does negative work. | The lost kinetic energy is not destroyed. | It becomes internal energy – the surfaces warm up.
T: Rub your hands together – friction makes heat.
@phys9-u4-q16
Q: How much energy does a 60 W bulb use in 2 minutes?
A: 7200 J
W: E = Pt with t in seconds: 2 min = 120 s. | E = 60 W × 120 s. | = 7200 J.
T: Change minutes and hours into seconds for joules.
@phys9-u4-q17
Q: Efficiency = useful output ÷ total input × 100%. Can efficiency ever be more than 100%?
A: No – a machine cannot give out more energy than is put in.
W: Energy is conserved: output ≤ input. | Some energy is always wasted (mostly as heat). | So efficiency < 100% for real machines.
T: An answer above 100% means input and output were swapped.
@phys9-u4-q18
Q: A machine takes in 400 J and gives 100 J of useful energy. What is its efficiency?
A: 25%
W: Efficiency = useful output ÷ input × 100%. | = 100 J ÷ 400 J × 100%. | = 25%.
T: The other 300 J is wasted energy.
@phys9-u4-q19
Q: A 50 kg person climbs 10 m in 20 s. What power do they develop against gravity? (g = 10 m/s²)
A: W = 5000 J, P = 250 W
W: Work against gravity W = mgh = 50 × 10 × 10 = 5000 J. | P = W ÷ t = 5000 J ÷ 20 s. | = 250 W.
T: Running up the same stairs faster needs more power, not more work.
@phys9-u4-q20
Q: A ball is thrown straight up from the ground (no air resistance). Compare its energy at the top with its energy at launch.
A: At the top it is all PE (v = 0); the total equals the launch KE.
W: Total mechanical energy KE + PE stays constant. | At launch (h = 0) the energy is all KE. | At the peak v = 0, so the energy is all PE: mgh_max = ½mu².
T: KE + PE = constant when only gravity does work.
@phys9-u4-q21
Q: Where is the zero (reference level) of gravitational PE?
A: Wherever you choose (h = 0); only changes in PE matter.
W: PE = mgh measures h from a chosen level. | Changing the level adds the same constant to every PE. | Differences (ΔPE = mgΔh) are unchanged, and only these matter.
T: Choose h = 0 at the lowest point to avoid negative numbers.
@phys9-u4-q22
Q: Express the joule in base SI units.
A: 1 J = 1 kg·m²/s² (= 1 N·m)
W: W = Fs, so J = N × m. | N = kg·m/s². | J = kg·m/s² × m = kg·m²/s².
T: Check with KE = ½mv²: kg × (m/s)² = kg·m²/s² ✓.
@phys9-u4-q23
Q: What is one watt?
A: One joule per second (1 W = 1 J/s).
W: Power = energy transferred ÷ time. | Units: J ÷ s. | 1 W = 1 J/s.
T: 1 kW = 1000 W.
@phys9-u4-q24
Q: What is the work done by gravity on an object that moves round a closed path back to its start?
A: Zero – gravity is a conservative force.
W: Work by gravity = −mgΔh. | Back at the start, Δh = 0. | So the total work by gravity is 0 (whatever the path).
T: Friction is not conservative – it always does negative work round a loop.
@phys9-u4-q25
Q: What does the area under a force–displacement (F–s) graph give?
A: The work done.
W: For a constant force, W = F × s – a rectangle on the F–s graph. | For a changing force, split the area into strips; each is F × Δs. | Total area = total work done.
T: Area under F–s = work; area under v–t = displacement.
@phys9-u4-q26
Q: A force increases steadily from 0 to F_max over a displacement s. How do you find the work done?
A: Use the area of the triangle under the F–s graph: W = ½F_max s.
W: A varying force: work = area under the F–s graph. | For a straight line from 0, the shape is a triangle. | W = ½ × base × height = ½ × s × F_max.
T: The same idea gives spring energy ½kx².
@phys9-u4-q27
Q: Set ½mv² = mgh and solve for v.
A: v = √(2gh)
W: Divide both sides by m: ½v² = gh. | Multiply by 2: v² = 2gh. | Take the square root: v = √(2gh).
T: Mass cancels – all objects falling from the same height reach the same speed (no air).
@phys9-u4-q28
Q: Trap: is any work (W = Fs) done when you hold a bag still?
A: No – s = 0, so W = 0, even though you get tired.
W: W = Fs with s = displacement of the bag. | The bag does not move, so s = 0. | W = 0 J; your tiredness comes from muscles working internally, not from work on the bag.
T: Physics work ≠ effort.
@phys9-u4-q29
Q: Can you add KE and PE? Are they in the same units?
A: Yes – both are energies measured in joules.
W: KE = ½mv²: kg × m²/s² = J. | PE = mgh: kg × m/s² × m = J. | Same unit, so KE + PE = total mechanical energy.
T: Only quantities with the same unit can be added.
@phys9-u4-q30
Q: To find only a final speed, should you use a = F/m with suvat, or energy?
A: Energy is usually shorter – it skips finding a and t.
W: The energy method links speed directly to height or work: ½mv² = mgh or W = ΔKE. | Newton + suvat needs a = F/m first, then v² = u² + 2as. | Both give the same answer; energy needs fewer steps when time is not needed.
T: No time asked, no direction changes? Try energy first.
@phys9-u4-q31
W: Force is measured in newtons (N). | 1 N = 1 kg·m/s² (from F = ma). | kg is mass, J is energy and W is power, so the answer is B.
T: N = force, J = energy/work, W = power.
@phys9-u4-q32
W: Dropped from rest: s = ½gt². | 5 m = ½ × 10 m/s² × t² = 5t², so t² = 1. | t = 1 s – answer C.
T: Check: v = gt = 10 m/s, average 5 m/s × 1 s = 5 m ✓.
@phys9-u4-q33
W: W = Fs cos θ. | θ = 90°, cos 90° = 0. | W = 0 – answer D.
T: Perpendicular force → zero work (e.g. a satellite's gravity in circular orbit).
@phys9-u4-q34
W: Δp = F_ext × Δt. | If the net external force is zero, Δp = 0 and total momentum is constant. | Friction (A) would change it, and "always" (C) ignores the condition – answer B.
T: Conservation of momentum needs zero net external force.
